# FIG-09-07: BFS in layers

status: draft   rev: 1   date: 2026-10-22   author: illustration spec agent

## 1. Summary

Two views on one sheet. View A draws the connected piece once, inside three distance bands, each of the five vertices carrying a chip that prints its distance. View B is the queue strip: six rows, one per frame of the trace, showing what the queue holds while A's chips settle. Read together, the invariant becomes a picture: the queue holds one layer, then two consecutive layers, never three values, and a vertex's distance becomes final at the moment that vertex leaves the front. MESSAGE = the queue holds at most two consecutive layers, and a distance is final the moment its vertex is dequeued.

## 2. Communicative intent

- AUDIENCE: a student revising for an exam, who can read Listing 9.1 and has met queues in chapter 5; believes BFS is a recipe whose distance array fills in some order the code decides.
- INTENDED CHANGE: believe that the layer structure is forced by the queue discipline, and that "already final" is a property of the dequeue event, not of the assignment inside the loop.
- READING CONTEXT: inside chapter section 9.3.2, after the sentence "The invariant explains the layer structure: when a vertex is dequeued, its distance is final, so the first time any vertex is reached is already by a shortest path." Column cap 1120 px, light and dark, phone read in the lightbox at 2x.
- REPRODUCTION: viewBox 0 0 1000 880, aspect 1.14:1, unit scale 1 unit = 1 CSS px at the 1000 unit reference width; displayed up to 1050 x 924 px in the article column; on a 390 px phone the sheet is read at 2x in the lightbox.
- LIFETIME: illustration system for the chapter, so palette, line grammar and node geometry are shared with FIG-09-01 to FIG-09-10.
- ASSUMPTIONS: 1. sample graph and distances from BRIEFING section 2 (confirmed). 2. canonical node coordinates with the single translation y + 70 (confirmed, BRIEFING section 3). 3. only the connected piece {`s`,`a`,`b`,`c`,`t`} is drawn; `u`, `v`, `w` are named in the caption as unreachable (confirmed by the figure brief, "the connected piece"). 4. the queue trace is recomputed by replaying Listing 9.1 on the chapter's adjacency lists, which moves `t` into the queue at step 1 and gives six frames `s`, `a b`, `b c t`, `c t`, `t`, empty; the frame list in PLAN.md (`s`, `a b`, `b c`, `c`, `t`, empty) cannot be produced from these lists (corrected, collected as Q1). 5. two views, one sheet, floors 30 and 26 from the roster (confirmed).

## 3. Goals and non-goals

- GOAL-1: the reader can name the band of any vertex from the chip, without reading a legend.
- GOAL-2: the reader can point at the step where a given chip changes role and say which dequeue caused it.
- GOAL-3: the reader can read the queue's contents at all six steps without stepping anything.
- GOAL-4: the reader can state the two-layer bound after one pass, because the note says it and step 2 shows it.
- GOAL-5: the reader sees that the empty queue at step 5 is the postcondition frame, not a failure.
- NON-GOAL-1: no shortest path tree, no parent pointers, no route highlight (FIG-09-05 and FIG-09-06 carry those).
- NON-GOAL-2: no discovery and finish times (FIG-09-08).
- NON-GOAL-3: no edge weights and no cost arithmetic (FIG-09-05).
- NON-GOAL-4: no second start vertex, so no second component (FIG-09-03).
- NON-GOAL-5: no code listing inside the SVG; the loop is in the chapter.

## 4. Constraints

- canvas: viewBox 0 0 1000 880, aspect 1.14:1, height declared here and in section 2; 1 unit = 1 CSS px at the 1000 unit reference width.
- node budget: 380 live marks, the two-view ceiling of BRIEFING section 4; the inventory reaches 83.
- palette budget: the nine role rows of the section 5 table, no hue added, no raw hex inside the drawing.
- typography: Montserrat, self-hosted. Canvas units: vertex id 18, chip value and step note 15, panel label 16 semibold, layer note 14. Nothing below 14.
- text split: prose in the chapter markdown. In-SVG labels are vertex ids, single distance digits, `dist 0` to `dist 2`, `step 0` to `step 5`, `dequeue s` style notes at most 12 characters, and one two-line note of 19 and 22 characters.
- convention: flat schematic, one projection, declared once in section 5.
- time appetite: the strip is the mechanism and gets the build time; the graph above is a static backdrop and is placed, not computed.
- detail: full
- element floor: view A 30, view B 26, per the roster; the inventory reaches 40 and 43 live marks.
- boundaries: Always state distances from BRIEFING section 2 and the trace from the replay of Listing 9.1; Ask first before dropping a band, a chip or a frame; Never a number with no ledger row, nor an em dash or en dash.
- claim status: distances (verified, BRIEFING section 2). six frames and dequeue order `s`,`a`,`b`,`c`,`t` (derived, Listing 9.1 replayed on the chapter's adjacency lists). two consecutive layers at most (chapter, section 9.3.2 loop invariant, confirmed on all six frames). seven edges in the connected piece (verified: degree sum 14 over 7 edges, BRIEFING section 2).

## 5. Visual system lock

- projection: flat schematic, one per sheet, no perspective, no isometric.
- line grammar: outline 3.2, edge and annotation 1.6, hairline 1.0, ratio 2:1 between outline and edge. No dashed line in this figure, because no hidden part exists. Arrowhead: filled triangle 14 long, 10 wide, on the dequeue arrow only.

| NAME | HEX (light) | HEX (dark) | ROLE | USED FOR |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| ink | #15181e | #e8eaef | structure and primary text | vertex ids, chip digits, panel labels |
| muted | #5c6470 | #a3abba | meaningful secondary | edges, step labels, step notes, pending chip outline |
| border | #e7eaf0 | #262b36 | decorative separation | plate frame, band edges, row separators, trough |
| card | #ffffff | #15181e | panel fill | chips, cells, plate and band base |
| wash | #f5f7fa | #111522 | grouping only | the two inactive bands |
| accent | #1d4ed8 | #7aa2ff | active, current, examined | dequeue ring, head cell outline, current row rule, arrow |
| accent-soft | #eef3fe | #16213c | active wash | current row fill, active band fill, head cell fill |
| accent-ink | #14357f | #c7d6ff | text on accent-soft | the active band's label |
| success | #157347 | #4ade80 | final, settled | settled chip outline and dot |

- value plan: three foundation values, card, wash and accent-soft, plus ink as the darkest text. The strongest junction on the sheet is the accent-soft current row against card, and it sits on the queue strip, the dominant element of section 8.
- contrast, from BRIEFING section 5, quoted so a builder cannot substitute a guess: ink on card 17.78 light and 14.77 dark; muted on card 5.98 and 7.7; accent on card 6.7 and 7.14; accent-ink on accent-soft 10.23 and 11.0; success on card 5.87 and 10.2; border on card 1.21 and 1.25. Two pairs were recomputed for the surfaces this sheet adds: ink on accent-soft 11.38 and 12.27, muted on accent-soft 5.38 and 6.9, so step labels and step notes stay on muted while they sit inside the current row. Consequence applied here: border and wash carry separation and grouping only, never a state, and no text sits on either.
- light: flat, no light source, no shadow, no gradient. Depth on this sheet is temporal, not spatial.
- detail floor: no mark below 1.0 units, no text below 14 units, no dot below radius 4.
- style reference: no frame rendered yet in this phase; the palette, line grammar and node geometry above are the style source, and FIG-09-01 will be the first rendered frame of the system.

